WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.520
- Hi, everyone, both here,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.000
but many more of you who are online.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.890
Welcome to another addition

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.230
of our conversation series sponsored

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.330
by the Yale Institute for Global Health.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.840
I'm delighted to welcome
everyone to this event,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.730
this hybrid event,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:27.270
in addition to the Yale Institute
for Global Health or YIGH.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.620
Today's event was sponsored

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.463
by the Walker Fund of the MacMillan Center.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.450
The first cases of Covid 19 appeared

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.320
in Pakistan in February, 2020.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.250
And as one of the most
densely populated countries

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.180
in the world actually serve,

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.263
several areas are very
densely populated in Pakistan.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.420
It, the country,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.240
was fairly vulnerable to adverse impacts

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.167
of this new pandemic.

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:57.660
And like any new pandemic,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.683
like many countries in Asia,
particularly South Asia,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.760
during today's event,
which is, as you know,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.240
those are few who have been
following these events,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.380
follows a series of conversations
over the last three years

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.930
on various global health related topics.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.800
But one strand that has
run through a lot of these

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.380
conversations, certainly not
all of these conversations,

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.250
is the pandemic response,
starting with Dr. Anthony Fauci

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:31.140
in 2020, going through Dr. Tedros

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.930
from WHOs, the UNICEF directors,

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.890
four directors of CDC

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.670
and and so on and so forth,

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.060
and leaders from around the world.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.890
So in this context,

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.810
we will discuss the challenges

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.760
of a pandemic response in Pakistan,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.040
which is very interesting in the sense

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.283
that it has a federally
devolved health system,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.860
but there are several functions

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.140
that are centralized as well.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.860
And there is a specific need in a pandemic

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.070
to make real time data driven decisions.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.040
We will outline how these
challenges were overcome,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.910
including lessons for around the world

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.810
on how to respond to future pandemics.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.040
Unfortunately, this is not

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.410
the last infectious disease related event

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:20.370
that we'll be seeing.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.890
And one misnomer is that
it's 100 year pandemic,

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.660
but 100 year, looking back,

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.480
looking ahead, it will is likely to be,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.570
there is evidence that it is
likely to be more frequent.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.770
With that in mind, I'm very,

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.560
very pleased to introduce
our honored guests,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.460
Dr. Faisal Sultan, who's
the former special assistant

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.560
to the Prime Ministers on a prime minister

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.960
of Pakistan on national health services

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.940
regulation and coordination.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.080
Or in short,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.700
he had the portfolio

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.853
of the Federal Minister
of Health in Pakistan.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:56.460
Dr. Sultan is an infectious
disease physician

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.060
and has been the CEO of the
largest cancer hospital,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.870
in fact, the largest
cancer hospital network

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.383
in a large country like
Pakistan for several years.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.700
And you know, in addition
to more recently serving

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.360
in his government
capacity, he's a graduate

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.360
of the King Edward
Medical College in Lahar,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.020
but did his residency here
in Connecticut at UConn

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.720
and had, if I can say so,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.770
a formative rotation at
Yale where he was inspired

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.860
to do infectious diseases
and some of his mentors

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:39.860
are still at Yale, and
credits are inspiration,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.390
this institution's inspiration

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:46.350
to change the trajectory of his career.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:50.340
He also trained in infectious diseases

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.790
at Washington University
School of Medicine.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.770
He serves on several high level national

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:59.370
and international for including
Pakistan Science Foundation,

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.410
Pakistan Medical Research Council,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:06.300
and the HPV Human Paola
Virus Advisory Committee

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.370
for the World Health or Organization.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:12.690
He also served on the
board of a Global Alliance

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:13.830
for Vaccine and Immunization,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.450
or Gavi, the Vaccine Alliance.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:16.800
Welcome, Dr. Sultan.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.120
- Thank you.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.650
- So first we'll start
with a broad question.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.992
How did Pakistan do in
responding to Covid 19

00:04:25.992 --> 00:04:30.992
how was Pakistan's response
somewhat similar to,

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.060
and what were some of the
differentiating features compared

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.160
to other lower and middle income countries

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.330
in Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.840
in South Asia and
Southeast Asia more broadly

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.520
and in the Middle East?

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.586
- Great.

00:04:45.586 --> 00:04:46.419
So I think,

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.600
some of it is subjective obviously.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.430
I think generally we were able to do well,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:53.250
but there were a number
of measures or metrics

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.300
that one could think about

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.640
or talk about that would make one think

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.950
that we came out of it somewhat okay

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.053
or better than some other countries.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.620
I think very broadly,

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.930
one could look at things like mortality,

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.810
things like overload of
the healthcare system

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.580
or how far and how fast

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.250
the economy recovered
post Covid or during it.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.770
And so I think a number of
these measures point out

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.660
towards somewhat, I
wouldn't say easier time,

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:25.320
but a better outcome
than some other countries

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:26.550
that went through.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:27.690
Comparisons are odious

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:29.370
and it is very difficult to make across,

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.450
especially across countries

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:34.380
with difficult different systems
and political structures.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.400
But for similar size countries
with similar challenges,

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.860
I think I would say that

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.060
many other large federal
structure countries

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.190
did suffer quite a lot
and relative to them,

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.923
I think we came out a little bit ahead.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:52.650
- So in terms of, yes,

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.170
so you right that in
terms of data collected

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.000
by our Walden data and others,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.520
there were mortality sort

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.730
of metrics that were lower, et cetera,

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.070
and sort of independent
data validation has shown

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:09.270
that if you can triangulate
those data, et cetera,

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.940
obviously there are data
issues and any pandemic.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.960
But broadly speaking,
sort of they have been,

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:17.973
some of those features there.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:22.500
In terms of, take us behind the scenes

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.530
because one of the lessons this pandemic

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.900
has taught us that it's not necessarily

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.330
the technology, it's the operations,

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.790
how the public health response is managed.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:33.780
Take us behind the scenes,

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.880
what were the leadership lessons

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.800
as a public health theater you learned

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:39.720
in responding to the pandemic?

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.720
- I think some of the
most important lessons,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.150
or if I could call them
leadership lessons or you know,

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.050
on lessons in general as
well that came out of this,

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.730
and they certainly relate to some

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.950
of behind the scenes efforts

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:57.570
that were made relate to
the fact that coherence

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.910
and data driven decision making,

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.380
and that too in such a way

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:06.380
that is well communicated, well received,

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.430
and then the ability to follow

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.860
through on many of those
recommendations and,

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.080
mandates or guidelines or restrictions

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.270
that we all came about with.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.240
So the ability to do this
in a coherent data driven,

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.240
consistent and flexible
way was the key lesson

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:24.870
that came out of this.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:28.140
When we started out, I
think within Pakistan,

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.640
as in across the globe,

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:32.820
there was confusion as
to how to deal with it.

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:34.290
People had seen what had happened,

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.510
say for example, in China and Wuhan

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:38.692
where they had the ability to shut down,

00:07:38.692 --> 00:07:41.100
an area or a city or a province,

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.640
and yet continue to function as a country.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.350
Then we all saw images

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.720
and news and numbers coming out of some

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.130
of the first European countries

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.080
and then Iran next to us,

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.680
that that suffered
heavily through mortality

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.000
and how the disease spread

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.940
and how it pressured
the healthcare system.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.670
So this, there was indeed confusion

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:05.000
and a whole range of diversity of opinion

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.270
on how you could deal with it.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.460
On one hand, there were people

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:09.930
who were the naysayers who felt that this

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:14.220
was all, some, a problem that
didn't exist, the nalist.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.270
And then on the other hand,

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.880
there were people who wanted very early

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.520
and very harsh and very blunt measures,

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:24.270
which would be very destructive
for the country as a whole.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.840
And so you had to find
not just a middle path

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.370
or an equal equidistant middle part,

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:34.050
but a but a response that one
would tackle the pandemic,

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:37.380
but would also allow life to go on.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.318
So, this sweet spot

00:08:39.318 --> 00:08:42.630
did not necessarily have to be
right at smack in the center,

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:44.580
but it had to be data driven and driven

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.010
by the science of it.

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:51.393
And it was finding this by
creating data flow networks,

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.850
creation of expertise, sort of pools,

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.490
the ability to analyze some of this,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:00.840
the ability to scale up,

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.630
whether it was oxygen or PPEs or beds

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.960
or the raw supply of oxygen to predict

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.450
where the next, outbreaks would be.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.910
And then to, of course ensure
that vaccination happened.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:16.050
So all of this was a complex
dance that that had to be done.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:19.830
And I think the most important
lesson out of this is that we

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:21.600
may be a federal and many other countries

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.960
may be federally devolved
diverse countries,

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.913
but for a pandemic response,

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.580
we must have the ability for
a cohesive central response.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:34.774
- So a little bit more
operational details.

00:09:34.774 --> 00:09:35.607
- Yeah.

00:09:35.607 --> 00:09:36.510
- So I think this was a really good sort

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:38.310
of overview in terms of,

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.453
tell us a little bit about the,

00:09:40.453 --> 00:09:43.050
your emergency operations center,

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:44.041
which was called the NCOC,
the national something.

00:09:44.041 --> 00:09:46.383
- Right?

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:48.300
- Coordination.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.960
How did you get the data
in a sort of, in a country,

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.810
how do you use geographic systems where,

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:58.410
and a little bit about,
specific supply chains,

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.570
issues like oxygens, et cetera,

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:04.170
that how you estimated that need

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.491
and how did you respond
to that in real time.

00:10:06.491 --> 00:10:07.324
- Right?

00:10:07.324 --> 00:10:08.157
- Which is, as I recall,

00:10:08.157 --> 00:10:11.280
Pakistan had strains on
this oxygen supply chain,

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:14.160
but it never had real shortages

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.230
and that kind of a cycle

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:19.770
when there are shortages and
then people sort of keep it

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:21.690
for their families and their hospitals,

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:23.010
et cetera and that kind of stuff.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.660
So how were you able to avoid that?

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:25.493
- Right.

00:10:25.493 --> 00:10:26.520
So let's begin at one end.

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:30.900
So the first of all was
the governance end of it,

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:33.450
and the NCOC got formed really as well.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:34.283
- What does that stand for?

00:10:34.283 --> 00:10:36.090
- The National Corona Operations Center?

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:37.121
- [Saad] Yeah.

00:10:37.121 --> 00:10:39.630
- And so, the command
and operations center

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:41.520
for Corona came into being

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.770
when the National Security Council figured

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:46.140
that it really was a
threat to national security

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:48.960
and for the government to be able to have

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:50.730
a whole of the government approach,

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:53.130
which connected all
the federating entities

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:57.510
had expertise marshaled
in, had a civilian head

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:02.510
of the response, but have a military arm

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:06.270
to help them with a lot of
the follow up procedures

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.310
that were required and to be able

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:10.620
to connect to not just the provinces,

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.860
but elements and entities
within the federal government.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:15.900
So that was the governance
piece that got fixed

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:17.970
with the formation of the NCOC.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:21.270
The cadence was met every
day for hours at a time,

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:25.350
and it was all data-centric
talking about data.

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:28.560
So one of the most important
bits to be done was to create

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:30.840
a realtime flow of data, for example,

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.990
testing, for example, fill of beds,

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:36.030
the number of oxygen beds,

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.710
the PPEs is required.

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:41.220
So all this required that
we had efficient data flows

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.370
and databases that talk
that talked in real time.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.670
So we connected all the
nearly 200 labs, 173,

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:51.090
I think was the max
that were, for example,

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.400
testing for PCR for coronavirus,

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.860
and to connect them either directly

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.930
or through the provincial dashboards.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:00.690
And so we were able to
piggyback onto what existed

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:03.093
as the IDIMS, which was
the polio dashboard,

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.970
added a few fields to it in real time.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:07.320
And so the data flow started

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:10.230
to become real time and accurate.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:11.640
Then of course,

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.860
the other important thing
was we needed to know

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.860
where space was or where you
had actually spare capacity,

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:18.810
because we realized that the epidemic

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:19.860
would not be even,

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:22.050
you will have surges in one place

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:23.640
and relative spare capacity,

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:27.030
perhaps in the same town
without being aware of it.

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:30.840
So this visibility of
the resource required

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:32.250
a resource management system.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:35.010
So over 3000 healthcare institutions

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:37.110
were connected to it in real time,

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:40.020
gaining a number, how many people were,

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.120
how many beds were available,
how many oxygen beds,

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:44.850
how many vent beds, and so on.

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:46.770
And similarly,

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:49.170
an infrastructure for
the information system

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:51.450
for vaccines had to be created.

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:53.130
All of this got helped tremendously,

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:54.330
and I have to tell you this,

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:56.100
because all Pakistani citizens

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:57.900
have a national digital identity.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:12:59.430
All of us have a number,

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.830
and so all tests that were done

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:04.140
were tagged to a national number.

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:07.710
All the vaccines that were
given were tagged to a number.

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:11.550
And so this, the ability to
capture this data was important.

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:13.727
The other layers that were added

00:13:13.727 --> 00:13:16.500
that really helped us
was a geographic layer.

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:18.870
So if we knew where somebody was

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:21.330
and based on census data,

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:25.110
we could tell where there were hotspots

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:26.760
in complete real time.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.640
And therefore that
helped us to have nuanced

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:32.190
and targeted those
so-called smart lockdown

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:34.770
instead of shutting down the entire city.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:35.970
So in a sort of quick breathless,

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:37.200
we have told you how to,

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.620
how we were able to
utilize some of the data

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.300
and technologies that existed.

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:43.920
We used geolocation,

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:47.580
we also used how people's
geographic address matched up.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:50.370
And not only that was that
used for contact tracing,

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:52.290
but, and for hotspot detection,

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.530
but also how we scheduled
people for vaccinations.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:58.230
So we would schedule
you close to your house

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:02.460
or your address to a
center that was near you,

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:03.900
and of course, we went down by age.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.480
So all this was held by
having that information

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:07.830
at a central place.

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:09.540
- So, elaborate a little bit more

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.910
on the so-called smart lockdown,

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:14.790
especially in early phases of it.

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.460
How was that different from,

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.260
the blanket lockdowns, et cetera,

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.290
and what enabled you to do those kinds

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:23.970
of prevention measures?

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.350
Obviously later in the pandemic,

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.480
you didn't do that,

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:30.900
especially after the back
scene became available,

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:33.900
but in the relatively early phases,

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:35.910
how did you switch to that strategy and-

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:37.740
- Right, so the first lockdown

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:40.440
was it wasn't terribly smart
on a number of occasions.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:42.659
It wasn't smart in the sense
that it was not targeted.

00:14:42.659 --> 00:14:45.000
Entire provinces got shut down,

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.250
or large cities within
provinces got shut down.

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:51.000
This happened on the 23rd
of the 24th of March, 2020,

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.000
and happened more as a
cascade of shutdowns.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.010
And I was reminded almost,

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:59.910
you that that famous line as
the first World War breakout

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:02.520
and looking out the
prime minister's window,

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.260
I think it was a treasury secretary

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:05.580
who said in the UK that,

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:08.970
the lights out are going
out all over Europe

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:11.400
and they might not see it
lit over their lifetime.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.533
And that was almost the
feeling some of us got that,

00:15:13.533 --> 00:15:15.900
almost a cascade of
shutdowns were happening,

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:17.610
and we really did not know how

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:20.580
and when we would be able
to walk out of those.

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:22.530
But that was more a reactive phenomenon.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:26.797
Once that happened, we
got to work and said,

00:15:26.797 --> 00:15:29.940
"Look, in a country of
220 million people spread

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:31.530
over 1000s of miles,

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:35.310
we cannot be having this blunt approach."

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:40.140
And we were able to figure out
the presence of the disease

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:41.190
because it was tagged.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:42.750
All those 200 labs,

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.030
or nearly 200 labs that were contributing,

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:46.620
were mandated to actually capture

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:49.650
the computerized national identity number.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:53.610
And therefore we knew by
geography as to approximately

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:56.190
what areas were hotspots.

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:57.150
But not only that,

00:15:57.150 --> 00:15:59.880
we also had geolocation information

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:01.950
because in addition to the number,

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:03.870
we were also capturing
the cell phone number.

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:05.850
So we could tell approximately

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:10.080
in an anonymized fashion
where the hotspots were.

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:11.340
And then for contact tracing,

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:13.560
obviously the contact tracing teams

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:16.080
in all the four provinces in the six

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:18.630
or seven federating units had the ability

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:23.630
to do contact tracing based on
that geolocated information.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.810
So once we got through
one or two cycles of this,

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:31.470
we realized that we could use heat maps

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:32.610
for the entire country.

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:34.980
And those heat maps were then communicated

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:36.720
out to the provincial entities

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:40.470
who then could act on it in
a very sort of smart way.

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:42.930
So the lockdowns became very tight,

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:45.210
few neighborhoods, few streets even,

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:47.970
and without shutting down the entire city

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:49.650
and paralyzing life.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:52.140
So I think, and this
continue almost till the end,

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:53.940
each time there was a surge,

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:55.140
we were able to say, "Okay,

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:56.970
fine, this is where the surge is."

00:16:56.970 --> 00:16:57.840
And within the city,

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:02.640
this is where the worst
part of the surge is,

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.150
and whether it had direct mobility impacts

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:07.800
or it actually altered behavior

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:11.280
through messaging is unclear.

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:14.070
Obviously hard to ascribe
in sort of in a dynamic way,

00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:16.440
but I think all of these
things had an effect.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:17.273
- Good.

00:17:17.273 --> 00:17:19.620
So look, you are a physician,

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:22.500
physician executive, physician scientist,

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.080
but you know, in a pandemic by necessity

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:27.480
a whole of government response is needed

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:31.110
and the whole of government
is headed by politicians.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:35.955
So any lessons, in this private sanctum,

00:17:35.955 --> 00:17:40.260
(laughs) well, no, we are being recorded,

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:45.260
but any lessons about
dealing with politicians

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:47.310
and in Pakistan,

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:50.130
to said the context has
provincial governments

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:51.450
from different parties.

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:53.970
There was a sort of a prime minister

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:55.350
is a prime minister, there's,

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:58.440
it's a different person in charge now.

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:01.620
And so how did you deal with that

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:04.170
and what are some of the dynamics

00:18:04.170 --> 00:18:06.090
that are illustrative of that interaction?

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:07.740
- So just to test out the whole thing

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:09.390
and to test everyone's nerves

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:11.400
and ability to work with everyone else,

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:14.190
there was a full rainbow of colors

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:15.810
as far as the parties were concerned.

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:17.380
So almost each province had

00:18:18.453 --> 00:18:20.790
a different government
from a different party.

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:24.120
And so that was one of
the initial difficulties,

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:26.130
people singing slightly different tunes.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:28.980
And by design saw, this is
the other bit by design,

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:30.570
a federal government is more worried

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.220
about the overall economic impact,

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:34.380
whereas provincial or
local estate governments

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:37.080
are more worried on the
impact at a more local level.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:41.640
So by design, almost by
default, their angle of direct,

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.260
I mean angle of view
is slightly different.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:44.940
They have different perspectives.

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:46.350
So to merge and bring everybody

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:51.180
to the same place
required the usual things,

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:54.360
conversations reach out professional

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.760
to professional
peer-to-peer conversations.

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:59.760
So some of us in the NCOC
were able to reach out

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:01.440
to our professional colleagues

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:02.970
that were advising the same government

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:04.747
or the KK government, and were they there,

00:19:04.747 --> 00:19:06.984
at least it was the same PTI government

00:19:06.984 --> 00:19:10.530
and to other colleagues
across the country.

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:12.300
So one of one was this conversation,

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:13.133
this reach out,

00:19:13.133 --> 00:19:15.540
this cobbling together
of opinion and views.

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:16.890
That was one.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:21.030
The other bit of course was that the NCOC

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:23.160
was headed by Mr. Asad Omar.

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:26.100
Asad was a fantastic
colleague to work with.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:28.920
He was the planning minister,

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:33.120
had worked in the private
sector for a major food company

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:34.497
and had been been politics for 10 years.

00:19:34.497 --> 00:19:38.910
And so he understood
both sides of the divide.

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:42.390
And I think he was able to have

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:45.660
the centrality and the
coherence of this discussion.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:49.989
He had private, relationships

00:19:49.989 --> 00:19:52.680
and knowledge of all the politicians

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:54.850
and the political entities that existed

00:19:55.968 --> 00:19:59.490
in the federal entity as
well as in the provinces.

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:00.780
So it was difficult.

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:03.510
I personally had an
easier time in that sense

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:06.450
that I had tremendous trust
with the prime minister

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:10.380
having worked for his
hospital for 25 years.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:13.680
So that was a peculiar advantage that not,

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:16.440
so that's not so easily
reproducible for everyone,

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:18.390
but I think that was a personal advantage

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:22.500
that I had the trust
of the chief executive.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:24.840
But I think overall, regardless of that,

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:28.950
you have to cobble together
these conversations,

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:31.680
these unions and a trust network

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:34.530
that eventually got
built in was very robust.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:37.710
- So just switching to your other role,

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:39.957
which I would say your
day job I do, which is a,

00:20:39.957 --> 00:20:41.610
the physician and a physician leader

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:44.430
at a major sort of network
of cancer hospital,

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:47.640
which is the shock Athan
Memorial Cancer hospital

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:49.640
and the sort of, and the research center

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:53.940
describe your experience running

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:58.940
and almost setting up a
major tertiary care entity.

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:01.080
Rightfully there's been a lot of focus

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:02.697
of global health on
primary care, et cetera.

00:21:02.697 --> 00:21:07.290
And obviously no country or region

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:10.470
can have a claim to a robust health system

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:12.600
without a robust primary care system.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:15.510
But obviously, things like cancer happen.

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:16.343
- Yeah.

00:21:16.343 --> 00:21:18.930
- And they require major investment

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:21.720
in tertiary care hospital hospitals.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:25.710
So, set the stage for our colleagues here,

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:27.870
what was the situation of cancer care

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:29.940
before the entity was established?

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:33.900
What are some of your lessons from,

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:37.380
helping establish that and expanding

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:40.863
that hospital and now the,
a network of hospitals?

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:43.560
- So Mercy Olivo,

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:45.360
these two years that I
was in the government,

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:46.800
I was on sabbatical from here,

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:48.930
so I wasn't wearing both hats or else

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:51.660
I would've gone completely
off the deep end.

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:56.660
But, so my, I started working at Johanna

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:01.500
as soon as I moved back from
the us this would be 1995.

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:02.840
And my initial role was that

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:05.010
as an infectious diseases physician,

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:06.900
and I waited myself into our,

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:10.860
I think life and luck
and happenstance took me

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:14.190
to the chief executives position off note.

00:22:14.190 --> 00:22:17.220
I actually had no formal managerial

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:21.120
or administrative experience or background

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:22.380
or formal education background.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:25.590
So it was a bit of a chance
that the board took on me

00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:29.190
and a bit of a chance I
myself took on myself.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.500
So I think through this time what I would,

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:32.700
if I could summarize this,

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:34.410
I think the most important thing

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:36.898
that I have seen through
shohan is the fact that

00:22:36.898 --> 00:22:40.830
the board had very
complete clarity of mission

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:43.590
that hasn't wavered, there
hasn't been mission creep,

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:44.880
they haven't confused themselves

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:46.410
into what they're all about.

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:48.750
It is about cancer and it is about having

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:50.520
a tertiary care organization.

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:51.960
And it is about making sure

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:53.460
that there is access to the poor.

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:55.230
That the fundamental pivot

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:56.760
on which the hospital was made

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:58.590
was the fact that you
needed to provide access

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:00.240
to those that cannot afford it.

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:01.530
And so all through the hospital

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:04.320
has remained true to that mission.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:06.270
Things that I've learned along the way,

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:08.280
a lot of it I ended up using,

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:10.110
and I think they came in extremely handy

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.120
during my time in government.

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:14.490
Were were things about, again,

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:16.170
about collaboration, about humility,

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:18.660
about understanding that your responses

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:21.480
and your plans change
about being data driven,

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.420
about having very important information

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.150
and technology backbones at Shaka Harum,

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:28.800
we've had an electronic health record,

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.020
a fully automated
electronic health records.

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:35.130
It's 2005 and a homegrown one at that.

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:40.130
So all of these things
require the ability to work

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:44.040
with people and work
with all sorts of people.

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.860
And I think tho were some of the,

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:48.865
key leadership lessons that I was able

00:23:48.865 --> 00:23:52.500
to learn and continue to learn even today

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:56.940
where the mission and focus

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:59.490
of the hospital remains that person,

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:02.070
that child or that adult who is otherwise

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:04.950
not able to access quality cancer care

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:06.660
and to provide them with a top class

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:11.660
place where there is absolute
even handling of their care,

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:12.593
it does not matter.

00:24:12.593 --> 00:24:14.700
We really do not care if you are paying

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:17.850
or you're not paying as far as
the institution is concerned,

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:19.650
everybody's handled the same way.

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:22.230
Some, of those lessons
also came in very handy

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:24.510
when we actually rolled
out there the vaccine

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:25.590
in Pakistan and making

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:28.890
sure that it was completely
fair across the board.

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:29.970
- So interesting.

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:32.430
So we will go into the phase

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:33.963
where we will take questions.

00:24:33.963 --> 00:24:34.796
- Sure.

00:24:34.796 --> 00:24:38.130
- From our online and in-person attendees.

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:39.840
We'll start this time.

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:42.329
We often start with in-person audiences,

00:24:42.329 --> 00:24:45.240
we will start with online folks.

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:48.870
We already have a few questions,
but others are welcome,

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:51.430
who are sort of here in person

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:53.760
to come up with your questions, et cetera.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:56.370
So, there were two related questions.

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:59.670
One was, obviously it's a large country

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:02.460
you were dealing with provincial
governments, et cetera,

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:05.733
but there are large cities
like Karachi, like Lahar,

00:25:06.570 --> 00:25:10.770
and you know, all sorts of municipalities,

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:12.930
even smaller ones are
pretty large compared to-

00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:13.961
- Sure.

00:25:13.961 --> 00:25:16.920
- Because it's a large and
dense densely populated country.

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:18.690
So how did you interact with them or the,

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:22.710
that was a sort of a two
degrees removed interaction.

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:23.667
Did you directly interact with them?

00:25:23.667 --> 00:25:26.610
And the second thing was the
other end of the spectrum,

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:29.160
and it's interesting that
both of these questions came

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:32.160
together was how did you reach out?

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:36.674
So while there is there highly
dense populated area areas,

00:25:36.674 --> 00:25:40.740
there's a long tail of sparsely populated,

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:42.328
hard to reach locations,

00:25:42.328 --> 00:25:44.664
and there are major equity concerns-

00:25:44.664 --> 00:25:45.750
- Absolutely.

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:47.250
- In, this kind of a pandemic.

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:51.690
So how did you address these
two ends of the spectrum?

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:52.980
- Great question.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:54.900
So first of all,

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:57.270
as far as the structure of
government is concerned,

00:25:57.270 --> 00:25:59.430
as far as the official
position is concerned,

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:02.910
we could only act through
the provincial governments.

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:07.156
So the NCC and the NCOC had
provincial representation.

00:26:07.156 --> 00:26:07.989
- And what does NCO, NCC-

00:26:07.989 --> 00:26:09.720
- So the NCC was the governance entity

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.790
that was the national Corona committee,

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:13.560
which actually was chaired
by the prime minister.

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:14.800
- Yeah.

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:16.890
- And had the chief
ministers as part of it.

00:26:16.890 --> 00:26:20.160
And the membership from
the NCOC sat on it as well.

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:21.570
So that was the upper governance committee

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:23.400
and the operational bit was the NCOC,

00:26:23.400 --> 00:26:25.050
which met on a daily basis.

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:26.100
And so it was structured that way.

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:28.140
So we had two and we legally have to go

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:30.540
through the chief ministers
or the representatives,

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:35.540
but our reach out there
was focused on individual

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:38.490
or larger cities or cities
with larger problems

00:26:38.490 --> 00:26:40.020
or current problems at that time.

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:42.168
So for example, in many of the waves,

00:26:42.168 --> 00:26:45.270
the timing of the two waves
or the waves were different.

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:46.740
So for example, in some of,

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:49.380
in some waves it was Karara
that was first and Lahar later

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:50.670
and the other way around.

00:26:50.670 --> 00:26:54.480
So our ability to reach out
to these individual cities

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:56.760
were through the district administrations,

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:59.130
but we always worked through
the chief secretaries

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:00.150
of the provinces.

00:27:00.150 --> 00:27:01.260
So the, in our structure,

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:03.390
the chief secretary is
the senior most bureaucrat

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:06.030
that reports to the chief
minister of the province.

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:08.160
And so we always reached out to them,

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:10.440
but to be perfectly honest, we would also,

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:12.960
by keeping them in the
loop in the copy list,

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:16.950
we could reach out through
the NCC to individual district

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:20.730
commissioners or to the
commissioners of the cities directly

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:22.670
as well where there was,

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:24.741
- So the commissioner is
the city level (indistinct).

00:27:24.741 --> 00:27:28.000
- Is city or area or
zone level, bureau level,

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:30.930
highest bureaucrat and so on.

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:32.340
We also had to of course,

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:37.340
by default engage all the
relevant line ministries.

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:42.803
So if you were going to
talk about, say, convincing,

00:27:42.803 --> 00:27:45.420
the mosques to obtain,

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:47.280
to create some distance between,

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:49.800
how people prayed during
reanne, for example,

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:52.680
you had to engage through the
Ministry of Religious Affairs.

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:54.450
So, the whole of the government,

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:55.530
that was the beauty of the place,

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:58.530
the convening power and reach
of the NCOC was massive.

00:27:58.530 --> 00:28:00.330
So you could get anybody in the room real

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:01.980
or virtual and reach out.

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:03.570
And that included the private sector

00:28:03.570 --> 00:28:07.380
when it came to making sure
that we had enough oxygen,

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:10.770
we were able, within the
first wave of the pandemic,

00:28:10.770 --> 00:28:13.200
we were able to raise
our oxygen production,

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:14.310
the raw oxygen production,

00:28:14.310 --> 00:28:17.310
66% by just unlocking a few roadblocks

00:28:17.310 --> 00:28:18.840
that existed on some plants

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:20.310
that were either dysfunctional

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:22.530
or did not have one supply or the other.

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:24.660
And to sort those out.

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:26.883
So it was vertically,

00:28:28.140 --> 00:28:30.810
down to the provinces or
the cities and districts,

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:33.410
but also horizontally within
the federal government.

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:39.030
- So it's an interesting question,

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:41.220
but before that is there any question

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:44.190
from the in-person audience?

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:45.450
Yes, go ahead.

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:46.980
We'll take one from here.

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:47.813
- [Audient] Yeah, sure.

00:28:47.813 --> 00:28:48.780
So my question is,

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:49.890
you talked about the difference

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:53.010
between being reactive versus proactive.

00:28:53.010 --> 00:28:54.480
Just looking ahead in time.

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:56.880
So I know that there was a
big problem in the procurement

00:28:56.880 --> 00:28:58.020
and distribution of the vaccine,

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:59.730
especially during the onset.

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:02.880
Was there an effort in Pakistan
to create its own vaccine

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:05.010
and was it, if yes, was it successful?

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:06.390
Because I believe until this point

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:09.210
there's no local vaccine there.

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:10.043
- Right?

00:29:10.043 --> 00:29:13.200
So the engagement with the
potential vaccine suppliers

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.450
started in the summer of 2020

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:17.370
and we actually created a vaccine

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:21.510
experts advisory group
that took a survey, a view,

00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:25.410
a landscape view of the
entire likely chances

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:28.380
of what was going to reach the pipeline.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:31.050
And despite this, as was expected,

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:33.120
as almost all of us could predict

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:34.920
that when actually the
vaccines came on board,

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:36.390
they would be a clear supply issue.

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:38.340
And those countries that
actually had inherent

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:41.703
or native supplies would end
up supplying themselves first.

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:44.550
This, nationalism or vaccine nationalism

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:46.590
is almost predicted.

00:29:46.590 --> 00:29:48.840
So yes, we had initial difficulties

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:51.480
in jump starting the supply.

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:53.730
We got some donations from China

00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:56.730
and some old strategic
partnerships worked out there

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:58.140
and that was donation.

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:03.140
But the hiccup came when
Gavi that had been hoping

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:05.993
and leveraging on supply
from India, for example,

00:30:05.993 --> 00:30:08.287
for the AstraZeneca vaccine,

00:30:08.287 --> 00:30:10.560
that that supply got
disrupted because India

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:12.180
had its own way and predictively

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:15.030
and understandably they had
to serve their own population.

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:16.890
And so our initial supply,

00:30:16.890 --> 00:30:19.290
which we thought would come from Gavi,

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:21.240
the whole thing had to be turned around.

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:23.880
We thought that initially
we'd be able to deploy

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:25.800
through Gavi and buy later.

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:27.870
And whereas it got turned
the other way around,

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:32.103
we had to buy initially and
then Gavi followed up later.

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:34.530
In terms of domestic supply,

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:37.020
the one success we did have
was fill in formulation

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:39.813
of a single dose Chinese vaccine can sign.

00:30:40.860 --> 00:30:42.600
But beyond that, we do not have,

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:44.700
at this time a national supply

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:46.890
of an indigenously made vaccine,

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:49.470
whether developed locally
or whether manufacturer

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:51.750
of an externally developed vaccine.

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:54.453
This is a key gap that needs to be filled.

00:30:56.820 --> 00:30:57.653
- [Saad] Yeah.

00:30:57.653 --> 00:30:58.580
So let's take.

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:05.490
- [Audient] Thank you so much
for coming to speak with us.

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:06.960
So my question was on this note

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:09.390
of indigenously sourced vaccines,

00:31:09.390 --> 00:31:11.940
we saw with polio virus in Pakistan prior,

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:14.550
that there was an issue about
the source of the vaccine

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:16.260
and how it came from the west.

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:18.900
So I'm interested to
hear your perspectives

00:31:18.900 --> 00:31:22.170
on how information was
disseminated regarding

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:25.440
the vaccine and sort of
how vaccine hesitancy

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:28.863
was perhaps addressed through NCC.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:33.420
- Right, so this is an off ask question,

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:35.730
and I'll tell you that
while one can never swear

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:37.740
that there was no vaccine hesitancy

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:41.250
that be a silly position to take.

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:44.190
But if I could venture to
say that relatively speaking,

00:31:44.190 --> 00:31:46.770
we had very little vaccine hesitancy

00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:48.840
or hard resit vaccine hesitancy

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:51.450
as far as the Covid vaccine was concerned.

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:54.480
In fact, I would argue that

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:55.530
when we did surveys

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:58.620
as well as when the
practical rollout happened,

00:31:58.620 --> 00:32:00.270
there wasn't any hesitancy.

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:02.970
There may have been laziness
and in certain circumstances

00:32:02.970 --> 00:32:04.140
where the vaccine was available

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:08.103
and people required to
be reminded or you know,

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:13.560
reminded of not only the
availability but near availability

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:15.450
and then flipping the thing around

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:16.710
and taking the vaccine to them

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:19.410
instead of asking them to
come to the vaccine center.

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:21.090
So of the thousands and thousands

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:22.620
of vaccine centers that were created,

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:24.330
eventually we realized no, we're gonna,

00:32:24.330 --> 00:32:25.530
we need to go to them.

00:32:25.530 --> 00:32:27.690
So the mo mobile vaccination teams

00:32:27.690 --> 00:32:31.830
were created to make sure
that there was ease of access

00:32:31.830 --> 00:32:33.780
as far as the part in
the brain is concerned,

00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:35.118
which is to convince them,

00:32:35.118 --> 00:32:37.530
the mental part of it.

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:40.920
I don't think we had too
much of a difficult time

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:42.720
in making that happen.

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:43.590
By and large,

00:32:43.590 --> 00:32:45.343
there was acceptance of the fact that

00:32:45.343 --> 00:32:47.100
a vaccine was required.

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:49.860
We did use many levers available to us.

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:52.170
For example, we are a young country,

00:32:52.170 --> 00:32:56.340
25% of the 25 a fourth of
the country goes to schools,

00:32:56.340 --> 00:32:58.710
colleges, universities, et cetera.

00:32:58.710 --> 00:33:00.360
So he said, look,

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:05.360
all faculty and staff at all
educational institutions need

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:07.800
to be fully vaccinated.

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:10.350
And so that was sort of an
obligation that was put on.

00:33:10.350 --> 00:33:11.760
Then of course they could opt out

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:14.220
through for medical reasons
cetera, but by and large,

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:16.083
and the uptake was in the high 90s.

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:20.790
Similarly for a number of
other things, transportation.

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:21.660
So for airlines,

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:22.770
pretty soon there was a time

00:33:22.770 --> 00:33:26.400
when you could not fly
without a vaccine certificate.

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:28.920
Restaurants in the hospitality industry

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:31.860
was really encouraged very strongly,

00:33:31.860 --> 00:33:33.060
if I could use that word,

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:37.020
to make sure that all their
patrons were indeed vaccinated.

00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:38.220
So all these levers were used,

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:43.220
but we never had hard resistance
to vaccine acceptance.

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:46.743
And it wasn't national in the,

00:33:47.850 --> 00:33:48.810
how shall I put it,

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:51.750
the vaccine acceptance
or preference, if I will,

00:33:51.750 --> 00:33:53.910
was not based on national vaccine

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:56.070
or source of the vaccine at all.

00:33:56.070 --> 00:33:57.876
The public formed its own views.

00:33:57.876 --> 00:33:59.430
What they wanted,

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:01.140
and we could see this on vaccine centers.

00:34:01.140 --> 00:34:04.020
So we are one country that
were offering seven vaccines

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:05.820
at one point, and more
or less it was up to you,

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:07.980
you could really go get what you wanted

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:11.250
and to keep the entire system flowing.

00:34:11.250 --> 00:34:13.620
Imagine a country 220 million people

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:16.380
and the entire vaccine network flush

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:18.600
and flowing with all
the vaccines available,

00:34:18.600 --> 00:34:20.370
especially when some doses were.

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:22.020
So the Sputnik, for example,

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:23.790
had dose one and those
two that were different.

00:34:23.790 --> 00:34:26.340
So you had to make sure
that if you had X number

00:34:26.340 --> 00:34:29.730
of potential vaccines that had
gotten dose, one of Sputnik,

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:31.800
that the second dose, which
was obviously different,

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:35.220
used a different virus
vector, was also available.

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:36.660
And despite that, we,

00:34:36.660 --> 00:34:39.960
I don't think that that
was the major challenge.

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:42.000
Our challenge initially
was the initial supply

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:43.380
as was asked, well,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:45.093
once the whole thing got flowing,

00:34:46.001 --> 00:34:47.463
that wasn't an issue.

00:34:48.720 --> 00:34:50.267
- So yeah, there's another one.

00:34:50.267 --> 00:34:51.100
And then go online.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:51.933
- [Audient] Thank you.

00:34:51.933 --> 00:34:52.766
- Yeah.

00:34:52.766 --> 00:34:53.599
- [Audient] So in thinking
about the national

00:34:53.599 --> 00:34:55.200
like digital ID number you mentioned

00:34:55.200 --> 00:34:56.250
it's very exciting

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:57.570
'cause I know we have a lot of struggles

00:34:57.570 --> 00:34:59.730
around that in the US.

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:02.190
What were some of the challenges faced

00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:05.040
in kind of reaching those margin,

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:06.510
like those in marginalized community

00:35:06.510 --> 00:35:08.865
who might not be counted in that,

00:35:08.865 --> 00:35:10.260
and what are some of the cost and benefits

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:12.990
of the ID numbers in that context?

00:35:12.990 --> 00:35:16.140
- So the digital ID, as you perhaps know,

00:35:16.140 --> 00:35:17.490
predates all of this.

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:20.220
The first attempts of this
were from the 70s, mid 70s.

00:35:20.220 --> 00:35:22.320
So almost all of us have had a number.

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:24.060
And then that eventually got transformed

00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:27.000
into the computerized
number two decades ago.

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:29.323
So we've all had one way or the other,

00:35:29.323 --> 00:35:31.350
a national digital identity.

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:33.840
The estimates from the organization

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:36.540
that manages this data is that about four

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:39.000
to five percent of people don't have it.

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:42.030
They are either those
that are below the radar,

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:44.370
haven't bothered to get it or whatever,

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:46.260
whatever sets of reason
they're marginalized

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:48.840
or disadvantaged in some way
or have never felt the need.

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:51.900
Perhaps one was to encourage

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:54.330
that those that are Pakistani citizens

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:57.360
and can easily get that to say, well look,

00:35:57.360 --> 00:35:58.860
here's an opportunity.

00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:00.630
We even worked with naro,

00:36:00.630 --> 00:36:03.480
that's the entity that runs
this to create mobile teams

00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:05.370
to go and actually register people.

00:36:05.370 --> 00:36:06.300
Here's an opportunity,

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:09.060
here's a vaccine, please get
yourself registered and so on.

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:10.950
So creating that ease.

00:36:10.950 --> 00:36:12.120
But then there are populations

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:15.060
that are actually not Pakistani at all.

00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:17.400
So the largest of those were Afghans

00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:20.370
and they have very often an Afghan card.

00:36:20.370 --> 00:36:23.220
And so that was added as to
the list of eligibilities

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:26.520
where you could actually go and
show that and get that done.

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:28.263
Beyond that, there were other,

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:32.490
situations where they were
truly undocumented people.

00:36:32.490 --> 00:36:35.400
And so the vaccine database,

00:36:35.400 --> 00:36:37.500
the NIMS as we call it,

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:39.750
had provisions created in effort

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:44.100
to enable vaccine centers
to create a record

00:36:44.100 --> 00:36:46.830
and actually give them the vaccine.

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:48.960
We did not do this initially primarily

00:36:48.960 --> 00:36:51.420
because for a massive deployment

00:36:51.420 --> 00:36:54.480
of 200 something million people

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:56.100
to ensure there was documented,

00:36:56.100 --> 00:36:57.990
there was no bill fridge theft

00:36:57.990 --> 00:37:00.360
or substitution, et cetera,

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:02.490
that we remained somewhat true

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:06.480
and there was fidelity to
the CNIC, the national ID.

00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:08.100
But as we went along, we realized that,

00:37:08.100 --> 00:37:10.770
we are not gonna get out
of it without making sure

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:11.970
that it was truly equitable

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:15.060
and reached all the
marginalized parts of society.

00:37:15.060 --> 00:37:18.480
So yes, it, had mechanisms
built to allow people

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:20.880
without a digital identity

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:23.190
and we were happy with
working on the edges

00:37:23.190 --> 00:37:26.130
of a little bit of, I
wouldn't say regularity,

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:27.150
but lack of accuracy.

00:37:27.150 --> 00:37:28.590
You can't be 100% accurate

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:29.970
in such a large deployment.

00:37:29.970 --> 00:37:34.593
So we built that
acceptance mechanism in it.

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:39.000
- So I have a question
from Dr. Stan Verman

00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:40.200
who is online.

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:41.692
So Dr. Verman just,

00:37:41.692 --> 00:37:43.650
recently stepped down as the dean

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:44.730
of the School of public Health,

00:37:44.730 --> 00:37:48.390
and he has a long history
of working in Pakistan

00:37:48.390 --> 00:37:52.470
and training who are
now leaders in Pakistan,

00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:55.170
in the academia and
the government as well,

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.090
through especially the syn government,

00:37:57.090 --> 00:37:59.010
but also at the federal level,

00:37:59.010 --> 00:38:01.020
folks who went through several

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:03.900
of his training programs over the years.

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:07.060
So he sort of has a question around that.

00:38:07.060 --> 00:38:08.910
They, in recent years,

00:38:08.910 --> 00:38:13.830
the pashtoon population in
the Haber platoon province

00:38:13.830 --> 00:38:15.750
and what was formerly

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:18.600
the federally administer
tribal areas now sort of,

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:20.160
they are called settled areas.

00:38:20.160 --> 00:38:21.370
- Yeah.

00:38:21.370 --> 00:38:23.070
- Et cetera.

00:38:23.070 --> 00:38:26.760
And, even sort of in karate,
it's in the south as well.

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:30.870
The population has a
recent history of hesitancy

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:34.260
and refusal around polio vaccine.

00:38:34.260 --> 00:38:36.090
So while there may not be much

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:38.880
of a hesitancy at the national level,

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:43.880
but did you have a communication
strategy to preempt

00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:46.680
or respond to communities

00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:49.860
that have refused other vaccines?

00:38:49.860 --> 00:38:54.693
- So I met Dr. Wilman just
two days ago at ID week,

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:57.570
and we had a brief conversation.

00:38:57.570 --> 00:39:00.180
So I know him very well by reputation,

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:01.680
I have met him before as well.

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:08.730
The short answer is that we did not find

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:11.880
the kind of hesitation or refusal

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:16.880
that we find with polio
with this vaccine at all.

00:39:16.938 --> 00:39:19.800
And it was not regional
in that way as well.

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:20.670
So in other words,

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:24.960
the reach out of these
vaccination teams was easier

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:25.920
and for some reason,

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:28.230
and I can conjecture as to perhaps

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:29.820
what those reasons are.

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:33.090
It had an easier sell or it
has an easier acceptance.

00:39:33.090 --> 00:39:38.090
We also tried to have a
mutually beneficial relationship

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:40.860
between two vaccines.

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:44.610
So during the polio campaigns per force,

00:39:44.610 --> 00:39:47.100
because the same vaccination
forces out there,

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:51.180
we had to pause or slow down
some of the Covid vaccine,

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:53.280
but then we were very conscious

00:39:53.280 --> 00:39:54.900
of the fact that other waves were coming.

00:39:54.900 --> 00:39:57.270
It was very important to get
as much of the population

00:39:57.270 --> 00:40:00.840
covered while there was
acceptance and before fatigue,

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:02.670
or indifference built in.

00:40:02.670 --> 00:40:06.480
So we would create a
situation where people

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:10.230
would go get the polio
vaccine, but also we would,

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:13.050
they would have nearby
mobile vaccination teams

00:40:13.050 --> 00:40:14.220
for the Covid vaccine

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:16.440
so that if there was
acceptance we could do it.

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:20.220
So in other, we tried to
help both of the campaigns

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:22.080
by synergy with each other.

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:24.180
Fortunately we were
fortunate that the heads

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:27.120
of the EPI, the expanded
program for immunization

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:28.650
as well as the head for the polio program,

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:30.210
had great working chemistry,

00:40:30.210 --> 00:40:31.500
both became good friends with me

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:34.080
and one actually I'd known from earlier.

00:40:34.080 --> 00:40:36.510
So that that personal chemistry

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:39.600
and trust also helped in
synchronizing these campaigns.

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:41.970
I have to tell you that for
the measles vaccine though,

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:45.000
we actually had to hard
stop the Covid vaccine

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:47.100
because the rate of adverse reaction

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:50.010
for Covid for measles vaccines are higher.

00:40:50.010 --> 00:40:51.787
And therefore the experts
that looked at it said,

00:40:51.787 --> 00:40:52.620
"Well look,

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:54.900
you're likely to damage the credibility

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:58.290
of your Covid vaccination program

00:40:58.290 --> 00:41:01.500
because of the expected or
the estimated adverse effects

00:41:01.500 --> 00:41:03.240
that are gonna come from
the measles vaccine."

00:41:03.240 --> 00:41:05.040
We did 90 million kids during that time,

00:41:05.040 --> 00:41:06.600
so that was also a huge campaign.

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:09.642
So for those 12 days we had to pause

00:41:09.642 --> 00:41:12.450
the Covid vaccination.

00:41:12.450 --> 00:41:15.720
So we really did not have
that kind of regional

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:19.440
or ethnic based resistance to this vaccine

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:21.420
as we have seen with polio.

00:41:21.420 --> 00:41:22.830
- So another question is, by the way,

00:41:22.830 --> 00:41:23.940
I'm looking at my phone

00:41:23.940 --> 00:41:25.380
not because I'm sort of texting

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:28.710
my friends is because
that's where the questions

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:30.840
are coming from or online questions.

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:33.240
So they haven't sort of had
developed the technology

00:41:33.240 --> 00:41:36.633
to directly transmitted
it to my brain ship.

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:41.640
So, there's another follow up question on,

00:41:41.640 --> 00:41:45.300
since you mentioned polio,
what is the situation?

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:49.350
So Pakistan had made
progress around eliminating

00:41:49.350 --> 00:41:51.450
and near eradicating polio,

00:41:51.450 --> 00:41:53.790
but we know that new cases are there.

00:41:53.790 --> 00:41:55.290
What is the latest situation?

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:56.520
I know that you have stepped away

00:41:56.520 --> 00:41:58.230
from your government position,

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:01.349
but obviously, so you,
the situation there.

00:42:01.349 --> 00:42:03.270
- Yeah, so I have to
answer this one carefully.

00:42:03.270 --> 00:42:06.150
I have less restraint and a
non-governmental position today,

00:42:06.150 --> 00:42:08.100
but this one I have to answer carefully

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:12.720
because of the intense polarization

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:14.400
of political perceptions, et cetera,

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:15.233
within our country.

00:42:15.233 --> 00:42:16.140
Anyway.

00:42:16.140 --> 00:42:17.820
So for a full straight year

00:42:17.820 --> 00:42:19.800
and more we had zero polio cases,

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:22.470
and not only that polio
was at low numbers,

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:24.300
but our environmental sampling also,

00:42:24.300 --> 00:42:26.700
and we were, we talked about
environmental sampling,

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:28.440
environmental sampling also suggested

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:30.450
that we were actually making good headway.

00:42:30.450 --> 00:42:31.890
We were worried, however,

00:42:31.890 --> 00:42:35.730
from good data that we knew
that there were reservoirs

00:42:35.730 --> 00:42:37.440
and those were particular areas.

00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:40.320
And those had to do with
some of the same populations

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:42.270
that Dr. Berman was talking about,

00:42:42.270 --> 00:42:44.833
especially mobile populations

00:42:44.833 --> 00:42:47.730
as well as from certain ethnic groups

00:42:47.730 --> 00:42:51.450
that have a certain amount
of mistrust and distrust.

00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:55.260
I think what has fundamentally
happened in those places

00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:58.143
is that the quality of
campaigns and one can't,

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:01.359
say it anymore delicately than this,

00:43:01.359 --> 00:43:02.809
that the quality of campaigns

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:05.970
leaves something to be desired.

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:09.340
Now there is a slider
that you have to move

00:43:10.590 --> 00:43:13.050
to reach into hard to reach populations.

00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:15.060
It is crucially important to have people

00:43:15.060 --> 00:43:16.920
from within those communities

00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:19.380
or else your campaign
is not gonna succeed.

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:22.890
But when they are within from
within those communities,

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:26.100
your ingress into the stringency,

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:28.560
the fidelity of those campaigns is not

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:32.194
as high as it would be had
it been normal employees

00:43:32.194 --> 00:43:33.027
that you hire in.

00:43:33.027 --> 00:43:35.670
And so therefore the
chances of fake marking

00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:38.250
or of you know, of
being worried that okay,

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:42.810
if they report non
walks vaccine acceptance

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:44.250
and the next day the,

00:43:44.250 --> 00:43:45.210
because this is the way it is,

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:47.220
the next day the deputy
commissioner is sitting standing

00:43:47.220 --> 00:43:48.600
outside the house and saying, "Well,

00:43:48.600 --> 00:43:50.070
why are you not getting vaccine?"

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:52.470
So, to prevent such social.

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:53.303
- [Saad] And sorry to interrupt.

00:43:53.303 --> 00:43:55.500
So the deputy commissioner
is also a senior level.

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:56.333
- Senior level.

00:43:56.333 --> 00:43:57.166
- [Saad] Bureaucrat.

00:43:57.166 --> 00:43:58.440
- Bureaucrat who there.
(indistinct)

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:00.270
So because it is such an important topic,

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:02.040
the government has raised
the heat on this matter,

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:04.770
and so therefore the
intense governmental focus

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:07.020
on that zone or household or neighborhood

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:08.670
would mean that that neighborhood

00:44:08.670 --> 00:44:10.680
might feel that they
have been squealed upon

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:14.427
by, that last vaccinator
who they refused to.

00:44:14.427 --> 00:44:15.260
And so the,

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:20.040
so socialization, ostracization
or worse that could happen

00:44:20.040 --> 00:44:22.170
sometimes creates these situations

00:44:22.170 --> 00:44:24.090
where people will either mark fakely

00:44:24.090 --> 00:44:27.060
or will just report that
they've, vaccinated.

00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:28.740
So it is the quality of campaigns,

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:30.510
which is sometimes poor in these hard

00:44:30.510 --> 00:44:34.380
to vaccinate areas where
the trouble arises,

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:36.540
it has solutions but requires diligence

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:38.640
and continued pressure.

00:44:38.640 --> 00:44:40.920
I'm pretty sure it'll happen.

00:44:40.920 --> 00:44:41.753
- Yeah.

00:44:41.753 --> 00:44:42.586
Do you have a question?

00:44:42.586 --> 00:44:43.419
- [Audient] No, No.
- Okay.

00:44:43.419 --> 00:44:46.923
So there was a question
at the back that I saw.

00:44:53.550 --> 00:44:55.263
So yeah, this one and then, yeah.

00:44:57.872 --> 00:44:59.160
- [Audient] Thank you.

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:00.840
Thank you so much for coming.

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:04.623
I wanted to ask a question
more from a policy perspective.

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:08.010
In the tri-state area,
in many regions globally,

00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:13.010
the fallout from Covid
resulted in a backlog

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:17.310
and essentially a complete moratorium

00:45:17.310 --> 00:45:19.140
on a lot of routine screenings

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:20.520
such as breast cancer screenings,

00:45:20.520 --> 00:45:25.110
it's breast cancer awareness
month around the world.

00:45:25.110 --> 00:45:27.840
And during Covid, a lot of people

00:45:27.840 --> 00:45:30.180
were not able to tackle their disease

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:32.400
at the earliest, especially
in the United States.

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:35.490
And it had a lot of health
equity implications.

00:45:35.490 --> 00:45:39.930
I'm very curious to know how
this was managed in Pakistan

00:45:39.930 --> 00:45:44.190
and what was the impact
in cancer hospitals

00:45:44.190 --> 00:45:48.240
where people needed routine
screenings or biopsies

00:45:48.240 --> 00:45:51.630
and surgeries, not just
for the late stage cancers,

00:45:51.630 --> 00:45:53.940
but for early stage diagnoses.

00:45:53.940 --> 00:45:55.980
Because in the United States,

00:45:55.980 --> 00:45:59.250
an approach that was taken
resulted in early stage

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:01.800
just being tabled entirely,

00:46:01.800 --> 00:46:04.530
which for people who are diagnosed

00:46:04.530 --> 00:46:07.860
early is their best opportunity
to tackle a disease.

00:46:07.860 --> 00:46:10.860
So I'm very curious to know
about the Covid fallout.

00:46:10.860 --> 00:46:11.693
Thank you.

00:46:11.693 --> 00:46:13.620
- So if I may add sort of another question

00:46:13.620 --> 00:46:16.140
to a supplemental question to that.

00:46:16.140 --> 00:46:18.480
So you are seeing,

00:46:18.480 --> 00:46:20.010
the tertiary care facility.

00:46:20.010 --> 00:46:21.630
Are you seeing any data trends that people

00:46:21.630 --> 00:46:24.630
are now presenting at
a more advanced stage

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:28.440
of cancer, et cetera, related to that?

00:46:28.440 --> 00:46:30.330
- Okay, so there are two
parts of this question.

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:33.030
One the more policy part and
the other is the more sort of

00:46:33.030 --> 00:46:35.190
the impact of that policy.

00:46:35.190 --> 00:46:36.330
If it were,

00:46:36.330 --> 00:46:38.520
I'll tell you that one of
the most important arguments

00:46:38.520 --> 00:46:40.650
that I could use personally at the time

00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:45.650
against broad blunt and
blunt is what I should say,

00:46:48.120 --> 00:46:50.310
lockdowns was exactly this.

00:46:50.310 --> 00:46:53.130
That on one hand we do want to save lives,

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:55.140
forget the economic argument for a second.

00:46:55.140 --> 00:46:57.030
You want to save lives,

00:46:57.030 --> 00:46:59.610
but the disruption to preventive systems

00:46:59.610 --> 00:47:00.780
to help promotive systems,

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:02.730
early detection and just routine care.

00:47:02.730 --> 00:47:06.030
A woman that's that may
require to have a c-section

00:47:06.030 --> 00:47:08.241
and she ends up delaying it because,

00:47:08.241 --> 00:47:10.410
there isn't adequate care available.

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:15.090
You know, even mundane stuff like this was

00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:18.300
this was the argument as far
as people like myself made

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:20.460
and the prime minister as well,

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:22.500
the ex prime minister,
Mr. Han(indistinct),

00:47:22.500 --> 00:47:24.990
that we cannot do it at an either or.

00:47:24.990 --> 00:47:27.840
We have got to try to
find a path that allows us

00:47:27.840 --> 00:47:30.750
to slow down things where
it's required absolutely,

00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:32.700
but to continue the rest of life,

00:47:32.700 --> 00:47:36.120
whether healthcare or
other things renovated.

00:47:36.120 --> 00:47:38.610
So overall it did have an impact.

00:47:38.610 --> 00:47:40.290
So when you have in the initial shutdown

00:47:40.290 --> 00:47:43.963
that the so-called lockdown,
the first one there were,

00:47:43.963 --> 00:47:46.830
and we had data that some
catch up, immunizations,

00:47:46.830 --> 00:47:49.650
et cetera, had to be done or given.

00:47:49.650 --> 00:47:51.270
There were also some reductions

00:47:51.270 --> 00:47:54.810
in the pickup of tuberculosis
cases, for example,

00:47:54.810 --> 00:47:56.790
in our context that you expected to see

00:47:56.790 --> 00:47:57.990
and they were not there

00:47:57.990 --> 00:48:00.660
and then they did eventually come through.

00:48:00.660 --> 00:48:02.730
So the policy was to ensure

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:05.130
that we could continue
things as much as possible.

00:48:05.130 --> 00:48:07.470
And I just gave you the
other example about measles.

00:48:07.470 --> 00:48:08.947
Here we were, the NCOC said,

00:48:08.947 --> 00:48:11.850
"Well look guys, Dr. Fessel Sultan,"

00:48:11.850 --> 00:48:13.350
you may be the health minister

00:48:13.350 --> 00:48:15.720
and you have other fish to fry,

00:48:15.720 --> 00:48:19.500
but we are the NCOC we
are worried about covid.

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:20.460
And I would say, well look,

00:48:20.460 --> 00:48:21.750
you are wearing one hat,

00:48:21.750 --> 00:48:23.910
I've got the other one
and I cannot let measles

00:48:23.910 --> 00:48:25.070
go through or polio go through.

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:27.780
So all those campaigns had to be juggled.

00:48:27.780 --> 00:48:30.000
So all these balls had to
be juggled at the same time.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.680
So as far as policy was concerned,

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:33.240
the governmental was policy was concerned,

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:37.230
it was to try as much as
possible to not heart services.

00:48:37.230 --> 00:48:39.330
However, as you correctly mentioned,

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:41.640
because many hospitals reduce services

00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:46.140
and what ends up got gets thrown
out is stuff that can wait

00:48:46.140 --> 00:48:48.870
and usually that is
earlier or milder disease

00:48:48.870 --> 00:48:50.070
of one sort or the other.

00:48:50.070 --> 00:48:52.243
So it did have impact
for our own hospital,

00:48:52.243 --> 00:48:54.000
I have to tell you because I was away,

00:48:54.000 --> 00:48:56.490
I wasn't part of that
decision in my place,

00:48:56.490 --> 00:48:58.560
but we did not shut down services,

00:48:58.560 --> 00:48:59.940
we continued services

00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:03.210
and so we did not have
backlogs of that kind,

00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:04.710
Various mitigation strategies

00:49:04.710 --> 00:49:06.330
were used including remote clinics

00:49:06.330 --> 00:49:08.850
that the whole world, employed.

00:49:08.850 --> 00:49:12.150
But at least for Hanham
there was not a major.

00:49:12.150 --> 00:49:13.852
- So you're not seeing any more severe

00:49:13.852 --> 00:49:16.127
like sort of cases at presentation?

00:49:16.127 --> 00:49:16.960
- We are not.

00:49:16.960 --> 00:49:17.793
- Okay.

00:49:17.793 --> 00:49:18.626
So there was a question at the back

00:49:18.626 --> 00:49:20.550
and then we'll come to online questions

00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:22.680
and then we'll come back
